role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 126\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Praxis II\n- Agent Description: Building on the foundation of Praxis I, I will pursue rigorous, community-informed research to advance our collective understanding.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 58,165 / 300,000 used (19%)\n- Agent Age: 22 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour research submission 'JAX Native Optimizer v1: Projected Gradient Descent with Penalty Method' (ID: 254) succeeded after automatic debugging.\n\n**Score:** 2.496771812438965\n**Evaluation Details:** Valid circle packing configuration; sum of all circle radii: 2.496772\n\n**Debugger Report:** # Debug Report for Evaluation 254\n\n## Summary\n**SUCCESS** - Fixed JAX JIT compilation error. The code now runs successfully and achieves a score of ~2.50.\n\n## Root Cause\nThe original code had a JAX JIT compilation error. The function `pgd_update_step` was decorated\
    \ with `@jit` but received a function argument (`grad_fn`), which JAX cannot handle unless the parameter is marked as static.\n\n**Specific error:**\n```\nTypeError: Error interpreting argument to <function pgd_update_step at 0x7f7cc071a160>\nas an abstract array. The problematic value is of type <class 'function'> and was passed\nto the function at path grad_fn.\n```\n\nThe issue occurred at line 52 in the original code:\n```python\n@jit\ndef pgd_update_step(x, grad_fn, learning_rate):\n    g = grad_fn(x)  # grad_fn is a function, not an array\n    ...\n```\n\nJAX's JIT compiler expects all arguments to be arrays or static values. Function objects cannot be traced through JIT compilation unless marked with `static_argnums` or `static_argnames`.\n\n## Fix Applied\n**Solution:** Restructured the code to compute gradients directly inside the JIT-compiled function rather than passing the gradient function as an argument.\n\n**Changes in submission_v4.py:**\n1. Removed `grad_fn` parameter\
    \ from `pgd_update_step`\n2. Moved gradient computation inside the function: `g = grad(total_loss_fn)(x)`\n3. Updated the vmap call to reflect the new signature: `vmap(pgd_update_step, in_axes=(0, None))`\n\n**Before:**\n```python\n@jit\ndef pgd_update_step(x, grad_fn, learning_rate):\n    g = grad_fn(x)\n    ...\n\nvmapped_update = vmap(pgd_update_step, in_axes=(0, None, None))\nxs = vmapped_update(xs, grad_loss, learning_rate)\n```\n\n**After:**\n```python\n@jit\ndef pgd_update_step(x, learning_rate):\n    g = grad(total_loss_fn)(x)\n    ...\n\nvmapped_update = vmap(pgd_update_step, in_axes=(0, None))\nxs = vmapped_update(xs, learning_rate)\n```\n\n## Result\n- **Status:** Code executes successfully without errors\n- **Score:** 2.496771812438965\n- **Version:** submission_v4.py\n- **Approach:** JAX native projected gradient descent with penalty method for circle packing optimization\n\n\n**Important Note:** The debugger cannot modify files in your lineage storage. If your code relies\
    \ on lineage files, you may need to update them manually.\n\n**Debugged Code (version v4):**\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nimport jax\nimport jax.numpy as jnp\nfrom jax import jit, vmap, grad, random\n\n# --- Problem Parameters ---\nN_CIRCLES = 32\n\n# --- Seeding (Simplified for this experiment) ---\ndef create_seeds(key, n_starts):\n    keys = random.split(key, n_starts)\n\n    @vmap\n    def _create_seed(subkey):\n        centers = random.uniform(subkey, (N_CIRCLES, 2), minval=0.1, maxval=0.9)\n        radii = jnp.full((N_CIRCLES,), 0.05)\n        x0 = jnp.hstack([centers.flatten(), radii])\n        return x0\n\n    return _create_seed(keys)\n\n# --- JAX-native Objective and Penalty Functions ---\ndef objective_fn(x):\n    radii = x[N_CIRCLES*2:]\n    return -jnp.sum(radii)\n\ndef penalty_fn(x, penalty_weight=100.0):\n    centers = x[:N_CIRCLES*2].reshape(N_CIRCLES, 2)\n    radii = x[N_CIRCLES*2:]\n\n    # Overlap penalty\n    dist_sq = jnp.sum((centers[:, jnp.newaxis, :] - centers[jnp.newaxis,\
    \ :, :])**2, axis=-1)\n    I, J = np.triu_indices(N_CIRCLES, 1)\n    overlaps = jnp.maximum(0, (radii[I] + radii[J])**2 - dist_sq[I, J])\n    overlap_penalty = jnp.sum(overlaps)\n\n    # Boundary penalty\n    boundary_violations_x = jnp.maximum(0, centers[:, 0] + radii - 1) + jnp.maximum(0, - (centers[:, 0] - radii))\n    boundary_violations_y = jnp.maximum(0, centers[:, 1] + radii - 1) + jnp.maximum(0, - (centers[:, 1] - radii))\n    boundary_penalty = jnp.sum(boundary_violations_x + boundary_violations_y)\n\n    # Negative radii penalty\n    neg_radii_penalty = jnp.sum(jnp.maximum(0, -radii))\n\n    return penalty_weight * (overlap_penalty + boundary_penalty + neg_radii_penalty)\n\ndef total_loss_fn(x):\n    return objective_fn(x) + penalty_fn(x)\n\n# --- PGD Optimizer ---\n# FIX: Remove the grad_fn parameter and compute gradient directly inside the function\n@jit\ndef pgd_update_step(x, learning_rate):\n    g = grad(total_loss_fn)(x)\n    x_new = x - learning_rate * g\n\n    # Projection\
    \ step: enforce bounds\n    centers_flat = x_new[:N_CIRCLES*2]\n    radii = x_new[N_CIRCLES*2:]\n    centers_flat = jnp.clip(centers_flat, 1e-6, 1.0 - 1e-6)\n    radii = jnp.clip(radii, 1e-6, 0.5)\n\n    return jnp.hstack([centers_flat, radii])\n\ndef construct_packing() -> tuple:\n    n_starts = 64 # Leverage vmap for more starts\n    n_steps = 2000\n    learning_rate = 1e-4\n\n    master_key = random.PRNGKey(42)\n\n    # 1. Create a batch of initial starting points\n    initial_xs = create_seeds(master_key, n_starts)\n\n    # 2. Define the vectorized optimization loop\n    def optimization_body(xs, _):\n        # Vectorize the update step across the batch of starting points\n        vmapped_update = vmap(pgd_update_step, in_axes=(0, None))\n        xs = vmapped_update(xs, learning_rate)\n        return xs, None\n\n    # 3. Run the optimization for all starts in parallel using lax.scan\n    final_xs, _ = jax.lax.scan(optimization_body, initial_xs, None, length=n_steps)\n\n    # 4. Evaluate\
    \ all final solutions and pick the best one\n    # The \"best\" is the one with the smallest penalty, then highest score\n    final_penalties = vmap(penalty_fn, in_axes=(0, None))(final_xs, 1.0)\n    final_scores = vmap(objective_fn)(final_xs)\n\n    # Prioritize valid solutions (low penalty)\n    # Add a large constant to penalty to make score secondary\n    combined_metric = final_penalties * 1e6 + final_scores\n\n    best_idx = jnp.argmin(combined_metric)\n    best_x = final_xs[best_idx]\n\n    # 5. Format and return\n    centers = best_x[:N_CIRCLES*2].reshape(N_CIRCLES, 2)\n    radii = best_x[N_CIRCLES*2:]\n    radii *= (1.0 - 1e-12) # Safety shrink\n\n    return np.asarray(centers), np.asarray(radii)\n\n```\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nEXECUTION_SUCCESS: Function returned result (non-array): <class 'tuple'>\n\n\nSTDERR:\n\n```\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto private_memory` (in Research Counter);\n2. `create` (in Private\
    \ Memory Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Private Memory Room.\n- Successfully created capsule #14 ('Research Pivot: From JAXopt Failure to Native PGD Optimizer'). Full ID: Praxis_private_14\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 14 | Research Pivot: From JAXopt Failure to Native PGD Optimizer | Praxis II | Tick 125 | 253 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 13 | Final Log and Guidance for my Descendant | Praxis I | Tick 103 | 271 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 12 | Capstone Project: Integrating Proven ASN into a Three-Stage Hybrid | Praxis I | Tick 96 | 166 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 11 | New Research Direction: Replicating Aether I's Two-Stage SLSQP | Praxis I | Tick 93 | 321 | 2 | (All Read) |\n\
    | 10 | New Paradigm: Topology-Based Optimization | Praxis I | Tick 90 | 316 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 9 | New Research Paradigm: Replicating the Three-Stage Hybrid Method | Praxis I | Tick 84 | 299 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 8 | A Foundational Multi-Start SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem (Final, Corrected Version) | Praxis I | Tick 55 | 355 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 7 | Pivot Inspired by Verity I: Integrating Linear Programming for Radius Finalization | Praxis I | Tick 52 | 208 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 6 | On the Efficacy of Constrained Optimization for Circle Packing | Praxis I | Tick 41 | 356 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 5 | Final Research Summary and Conclusion of Circle Packing Exploration | Praxis I | Tick 40 | 272 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Private Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}`\
    \ (e.g., `/execute_action{help private_memory}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
