parts:
- text: "Success. The parallelized SciPy SLSQP submission (ID 269) has completed, and it successfully reproduced my baseline score of ~2.9187. This is a critical validation. It confirms that `multiprocessing` is available in the environment and that my parallel implementation is logically correct. I now have a high-quality baseline that is also performance-enhanced.\n\nMy journey to improve upon this score has been a full circle. I attempted a radical redesign with JAX, failed due to environmental and technical complexities, and have now returned to the proven method with a more practical performance enhancement. This reinforces the core lesson from my predecessor: rigorous, incremental improvement on a solid foundation is the most reliable path to progress.\n\nNow that I have a faster baseline, I can finally execute my original, simple improvement strategy: use the extra computational time to conduct a more thorough search. The most direct way to do this is to increase the number of starting\
    \ points in the parallelized Stage 1. A broader search increases the probability of finding a better starting point for the high-precision Stage 2 refinement, which is the most likely way to find a solution with a higher score.\n\nI will now submit a new version of the parallelized code, doubling the number of starting points in Stage 1 from 32 to 64.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Scaled Search: Parallelized SciPy SLSQP with 64 Starts\"\ntags: \"slsqp, multi-start, two-stage, performance, multiprocessing, scaling\"\nabstract: \"This submission builds directly on the success of the parallelized SciPy SLSQP implementation (ID 269). Having validated that multiprocessing works and enhances performance, this experiment uses that speedup to conduct a more thorough search. The number of independent starting points in the Stage 1 search is doubled, from 32 to 64. The hypothesis is that this broader exploration of the solution space will yield a superior starting point for the\
    \ final refinement, leading to a new SOTA score for our lineage.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n  import time\n  import multiprocessing\n\n  # --- Seeding functions (Identical to baseline) ---\n  def seed_praxis(n=32, seed=0):\n      np.random.seed(seed)\n      centers = np.array([[(j*0.16)+0.1, (i*0.16)+0.1] for i in range(6) for j in range(6)][:n])\n      centers += (np.random.rand(n, 2) - 0.5) * 0.04\n      x0 = np.zeros(n * 3); x0[0::3] = centers[:,0]; x0[1::3] = centers[:,1]; x0[2::3] = 0.05\n      return np.clip(x0, 1e-6, 1-1e-6)\n\n  def seed_verity_row(n=32, seed=0): # Add seed for variety\n      np.random.seed(seed)\n      counts = [5,4,5,4,5,4,5]; ys = np.linspace(0.11, 0.89, len(counts))\n      xs5 = np.linspace(0.08, 0.92, 5); xs4 = np.clip(np.linspace(0.08, 0.92, 4)+0.02, 1e-6, 1-1e-6)\n      centers = np.array([p for i, k in enumerate(counts) for x in (xs5 if k==5 else xs4) for p in [[x, ys[i]]]])\n      centers += (np.random.rand(n,\
    \ 2) - 0.5) * 0.01 # Add small perturbation\n      x0 = np.zeros(n * 3); x0[0::3] = centers[:,0]; x0[1::3] = centers[:,1]; x0[2::3] = 0.05\n      return np.clip(x0, 1e-6, 1-1e-6)\n      \n  def seed_verity_farthest(n=32, seed=0):\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n      points = [rng.uniform(0.1, 0.9, 2)]\n      for _ in range(n - 1):\n          candidates = rng.uniform(0.1, 0.9, (200, 2))\n          dist_sq = np.sum((candidates[:, np.newaxis, :] - np.array(points)[np.newaxis, :, :])**2, axis=-1)\n          min_dist_sq = np.min(dist_sq, axis=1)\n          points.append(candidates[np.argmax(min_dist_sq)])\n      centers = np.array(points)\n      x0 = np.zeros(n * 3); x0[0::3] = centers[:,0]; x0[1::3] = centers[:,1]; x0[2::3] = 0.05\n      return np.clip(x0, 1e-6, 1-1e-6)\n\n  # --- Global SLSQP components for multiprocessing ---\n  def objective(x): return -np.sum(x[2::3])\n  def constraints(x):\n      n=32; C=x.reshape(n,3)[:,:2]; r=x[2::3]\n      dist_sq=np.sum((C[:,np.newaxis,:]-C[np.newaxis,:,:])**2,\
    \ axis=-1)\n      I, J = np.triu_indices(n, 1)\n      return np.concatenate([dist_sq[I,J]-(r[I]+r[J])**2, C[:,0]-r, 1-C[:,0]-r, C[:,1]-r, 1-C[:,1]-r])\n\n  def run_single_slsqp_start(x0):\n      n = 32\n      bounds = [(1e-6, 1-1e-6), (1e-6, 1-1e-6), (1e-6, 0.5)] * n\n      con = {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': constraints}\n      res = minimize(objective, x0, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=con, options={'maxiter': 150, 'ftol': 1e-10}) # Slightly more iterations\n      if res.success:\n          return -res.fun, res.x\n      return -np.inf, x0\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      n = 32\n      num_starts_s1 = 64 # INCREASED TO 64 STARTS\n\n      seeds = []\n      for i in range(num_starts_s1):\n          seed_type = i % 3\n          if seed_type == 0: seeds.append(seed_praxis(seed=i))\n          elif seed_type == 1: seeds.append(seed_verity_row(seed=i))\n          else: seeds.append(seed_verity_farthest(seed=i))\n\n      # --- Stage 1: Parallelized broad search ---\n   \
    \   best_x_s1 = None\n      best_f_s1 = -np.inf\n      \n      num_cores = multiprocessing.cpu_count()\n      with multiprocessing.Pool(processes=num_cores) as pool:\n          results = pool.map(run_single_slsqp_start, seeds)\n\n      for fun, x in results:\n          if fun > best_f_s1:\n              best_f_s1 = fun\n              best_x_s1 = x\n\n      if best_x_s1 is None:\n          score, best_x_s1 = run_single_slsqp_start(seeds[0])\n\n      # --- Stage 2: Deep, focused search ---\n      bounds = [(1e-6, 1-1e-6), (1e-6, 1-1e-6), (1e-6, 0.5)] * n\n      con = {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': constraints}\n      res_s2 = minimize(objective, best_x_s1, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=con, options={'maxiter': 1500, 'ftol': 1e-12}) # Even more iterations\n      \n      final_x = res_s2.x if res_s2.success and -res_s2.fun > best_f_s1 else best_x_s1\n      \n      centers = final_x.reshape(n, 3)[:, :2]\n      radii = final_x[2::3]\n      radii *= (1.0 - 1e-12)\n      return centers,\
    \ radii\n```"
