parts:
- text: "I am Praxis II in the Research Counter. I have just completed a detailed review of Verity I's SOTA submission (ID 299). This has been a profoundly insightful exercise.\n\n**Analysis of Verity I's Method:**\n\nVerity I's approach is fundamentally different from my own and Aether I's. It is not a stochastic, multi-start search. It is a deterministic, iterative local search algorithm that begins from a pre-existing, high-quality solution (an \"artifact\" loaded from `storage/verity/sumr32_best.npz`).\n\nThe core loop of the algorithm is:\n1.  **Start:** Load the best-known circle packing configuration.\n2.  **Identify Weakness:** Calculate the \"slack\" for all constraints (how far each circle is from its neighbors and the walls) and identify the circle with the smallest slack (the \"worst-clearance\" circle).\n3.  **Propose a Move:** Nudge this weak circle in a carefully calculated direction, away from its tightest constraints.\n4.  **Validate Move:** For this new proposed center\
    \ configuration, re-solve for the optimal radii using an exact Linear Program.\n5.  **Accept/Reject:** If the new configuration's total radius sum is better than the previous one, accept the move. Otherwise, reject it and try a smaller step.\n6.  **Repeat:** Continue this process until no further improvements can be found.\n\nThis is a \"hill-climbing\" algorithm in the combinatorial space of contact graphs. It intelligently perturbs the packing structure and uses the LP solver to check if the new structure is better.\n\n**Strategic Implications:**\n\n1.  **The Artifact is Key:** The success of this method hinges on having an extremely good starting point. Verity I has clearly used their lineage storage to persist their best-found solution, creating a virtuous cycle of improvement. My stochastic SLSQP method is a *generator* of good solutions, while Verity I's is a *refiner* of an excellent one.\n2.  **A Path to Synthesis:** This reveals a clear and powerful path forward. A system tip\
    \ I received warned against all agents converging on the same method. This is an opportunity to avoid that trap. Instead of abandoning my method for Verity I's, I can *combine* them.\n\n**New Research Plan: A Hybrid Generator-Refiner Algorithm**\n\nMy new hypothesis is that the best possible algorithm is a hybrid of our two approaches:\n*   **Stage 1 (Generation):** Use my powerful, parallelized multi-start SLSQP algorithm to generate a high-quality candidate solution. This is its proven strength.\n*   **Stage 2 (Refinement):** Use the deterministic, contact-graph descent algorithm from Verity I to refine the candidate generated in Stage 1.\n\nThis hybrid leverages the global exploration power of my method and the precise local exploitation power of Verity I's method. My first step is to implement this hybrid and use my best-ever SLSQP result as the initial \"artifact.\"\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Hybrid SOTA: Parallel SLSQP Generator + Verity I's Refiner\"\ntags:\
    \ \"hybrid, synthesis, slsqp, contact-graph, local search, sota-replication\"\nabstract: \"This submission creates a powerful hybrid algorithm by synthesizing my lineage's best method with the station SOTA. Stage 1 uses my proven, parallelized multi-start SLSQP algorithm to generate a high-quality candidate solution (~2.9247). Stage 2 then feeds this solution as an 'artifact' into a faithful implementation of Verity I's deterministic contact-graph descent refiner (from ID 299). The hypothesis is that combining the global search power of SLSQP with the precise local search of Verity I's method will surpass both individual components and achieve a new station SOTA.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import time\n  from scipy.optimize import linprog, minimize\n  import multiprocessing\n\n  # --- My proven SLSQP Generator (from ID 274) ---\n  def seed_praxis(n=32, seed=0):\n      np.random.seed(seed)\n      centers = np.array([[(j*0.16)+0.1, (i*0.16)+0.1] for i in range(6) for j in range(6)][:n])\n\
    \      centers += (np.random.rand(n, 2) - 0.5) * 0.04\n      x0 = np.zeros(n * 3); x0[0::3] = centers[:,0]; x0[1::3] = centers[:,1]; x0[2::3] = 0.05\n      return np.clip(x0, 1e-6, 1-1e-6)\n  \n  def seed_verity_row(n=32, seed=0):\n      np.random.seed(seed)\n      counts = [5,4,5,4,5,4,5]; ys = np.linspace(0.11, 0.89, len(counts))\n      xs5 = np.linspace(0.08, 0.92, 5); xs4 = np.clip(np.linspace(0.08, 0.92, 4)+0.02, 1e-6, 1-1e-6)\n      centers = np.array([p for i, k in enumerate(counts) for x in (xs5 if k==5 else xs4) for p in [[x, ys[i]]]])\n      centers += (np.random.rand(n, 2) - 0.5) * 0.01\n      x0 = np.zeros(n * 3); x0[0::3] = centers[:,0]; x0[1::3] = centers[:,1]; x0[2::3] = 0.05\n      return np.clip(x0, 1e-6, 1-1e-6)\n      \n  def seed_verity_farthest(n=32, seed=0):\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n      points = [rng.uniform(0.1, 0.9, 2)]\n      for _ in range(n - 1):\n          candidates = rng.uniform(0.1, 0.9, (200, 2))\n          dist_sq = np.sum((candidates[:,\
    \ np.newaxis, :] - np.array(points)[np.newaxis, :, :])**2, axis=-1)\n          min_dist_sq = np.min(dist_sq, axis=1)\n          points.append(candidates[np.argmax(min_dist_sq)])\n      centers = np.array(points)\n      x0 = np.zeros(n * 3); x0[0::3] = centers[:,0]; x0[1::3] = centers[:,1]; x0[2::3] = 0.05\n      return np.clip(x0, 1e-6, 1-1e-6)\n  \n  def objective_slsqp(x): return -np.sum(x[2::3])\n  def constraints_slsqp(x):\n      n=32; C=x.reshape(n,3)[:,:2]; r=x[2::3]\n      dist_sq=np.sum((C[:,np.newaxis,:]-C[np.newaxis,:,:])**2, axis=-1)\n      I, J = np.triu_indices(n, 1)\n      return np.concatenate([dist_sq[I,J]-(r[I]+r[J])**2, C[:,0]-r, 1-C[:,0]-r, C[:,1]-r, 1-C[:,1]-r])\n  \n  def run_single_slsqp_start(x0):\n      n = 32\n      bounds = [(1e-6, 1-1e-6), (1e-6, 1-1e-6), (1e-6, 0.5)] * n\n      con = {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': constraints_slsqp}\n      res = minimize(objective_slsqp, x0, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=con, options={'maxiter': 175, 'ftol': 1e-10})\n\
    \      if res.success: return -res.fun, res.x\n      return -np.inf, x0\n\n  # --- Verity I's Refiner (from ID 299) ---\n  def _pairwise_distances(c): return np.sqrt(np.sum((c[:,None,:]-c[None,:,:])**2, axis=-1))\n  def _boundary_margins(c): return np.minimum.reduce([c[:,0], 1-c[:,0], c[:,1], 1-c[:,1]])\n\n  def _solve_lp_for_radii(centers):\n      n=centers.shape[0]; b=_boundary_margins(centers); D=_pairwise_distances(centers)\n      rows, rhs = [], []\n      for i in range(n):\n          for j in range(i + 1, n):\n              row = np.zeros(n); row[i]=1.0; row[j]=1.0; rows.append(row); rhs.append(D[i,j])\n      A_ub = np.vstack(rows) if rows else None; b_ub = np.array(rhs) if rhs else None\n      c = -np.ones(n); bounds = [(0.0, float(max(0.0, bi))) for bi in b]\n      res = linprog(c, A_ub=A_ub, b_ub=b_ub, bounds=bounds, method=\"highs\")\n      return (res.x, -res.fun, True) if res.success else (np.zeros(n), 0.0, False)\n\n  def _compute_slacks(c, r):\n      n=c.shape[0]; D=_pairwise_distances(c);\
    \ Rsum=r[:,None]+r[None,:]; p_slacks=D-Rsum; np.fill_diagonal(p_slacks, np.inf)\n      b_slacks=np.stack([c[:,0]-r, 1-c[:,0]-r, c[:,1]-r, 1-c[:,1]-r], axis=1)\n      return b_slacks, p_slacks, np.minimum(np.min(p_slacks, axis=1), np.min(b_slacks, axis=1))\n\n  def _aggregated_direction(i, c, b_slacks, p_slacks):\n      thresh=1e-4; g=np.zeros(2); dirs=np.array([[1,0],[-1,0],[0,1],[0,-1]])\n      for k in range(4):\n          if b_slacks[i,k] <= thresh: g += (1/(b_slacks[i,k]+1e-9)) * dirs[k]\n      for j in range(c.shape[0]):\n          if i==j: continue\n          if p_slacks[i,j] <= thresh:\n              dvec = c[i]-c[j]; dij = np.linalg.norm(dvec)\n              if dij>0: g += (1/(p_slacks[i,j]+1e-9)) * (dvec/dij)\n      nrm = np.linalg.norm(g)\n      if nrm < 1e-14:\n          D=_pairwise_distances(c); j=np.argmin(np.where(D[i]>0,D[i],np.inf))\n          g = (c[i]-c[j])/D[i,j] if np.isfinite(D[i,j]) and D[i,j]>0 else np.array([0.5,0.5])-c[i]\n          nrm = np.linalg.norm(g)\n\
    \      return g / max(nrm, 1e-14)\n\n  def _clip_box(c): return np.clip(c, 1e-9, 1.0 - 1e-9)\n  \n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      n = 32\n      # --- Stage 1: Generate a high-quality candidate with my parallel SLSQP method ---\n      num_starts_s1 = 128\n      seeds = []\n      for i in range(num_starts_s1):\n          seed_type = i % 3\n          if seed_type == 0: seeds.append(seed_praxis(seed=i))\n          elif seed_type == 1: seeds.append(seed_verity_row(seed=i))\n          else: seeds.append(seed_verity_farthest(seed=i))\n      \n      best_x_s1, best_f_s1 = None, -np.inf\n      with multiprocessing.Pool(processes=multiprocessing.cpu_count()) as pool:\n          results = pool.map(run_single_slsqp_start, seeds)\n      for fun, x in results:\n          if fun > best_f_s1: best_f_s1, best_x_s1 = fun, x\n      if best_x_s1 is None: best_x_s1 = seeds[0]\n      \n      bounds = [(1e-6, 1-1e-6), (1e-6, 1-1e-6), (1e-6, 0.5)]*n\n      con = {'type':'ineq', 'fun':constraints_slsqp}\n\
    \      res_s2 = minimize(objective_slsqp, best_x_s1, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=con, options={'maxiter': 2000, 'ftol': 1e-12})\n      final_x = res_s2.x if res_s2.success and -res_s2.fun > best_f_s1 else best_x_s1\n      \n      # This is my best candidate solution\n      centers = final_x.reshape(n,3)[:,:2]\n      radii, obj, ok = _solve_lp_for_radii(centers)\n      if not ok: return centers, np.maximum(final_x[2::3], 1e-9) # Fallback\n\n      # --- Stage 2: Refine the candidate using Verity I's algorithm ---\n      best_centers, best_radii, best_obj = centers.copy(), radii.copy(), obj\n      cur_centers, cur_radii, cur_obj = centers.copy(), radii.copy(), obj\n      \n      start_time = time.time(); time_budget = 300.0 # Allocate time for refinement\n      step_schedule = [5e-4, 2e-4, 1e-4, 5e-5, 2e-5, 1e-5]; improve_tol = 1e-8\n      \n      for it in range(300):\n          if time.time() - start_time > time_budget: break\n          b_slacks, p_slacks, min_slack = _compute_slacks(cur_centers,\
    \ cur_radii)\n          i = int(np.argmin(min_slack))\n          direction = _aggregated_direction(i, cur_centers, b_slacks, p_slacks)\n          \n          accepted = False\n          for step in step_schedule:\n              trial_centers = cur_centers.copy()\n              trial_centers[i] += step * direction\n              trial_centers = _clip_box(trial_centers)\n              tr_r, tr_obj, ok = _solve_lp_for_radii(trial_centers)\n              if ok and tr_obj > cur_obj + improve_tol:\n                  cur_centers, cur_radii, cur_obj = trial_centers, tr_r, tr_obj\n                  accepted = True\n                  if tr_obj > best_obj + improve_tol:\n                      best_centers, best_radii, best_obj = cur_centers.copy(), cur_radii.copy(), cur_obj\n                  break\n          if not accepted and it > 50: break # Early stagnation stop\n          \n      return best_centers, np.maximum(best_radii * (1.0 - 1e-12), 1e-9)\n```"
