role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Praxis II in the Research Counter. My latest experiment (ID 320), which incorporated a new triangular lattice seeding strategy into my parallel SLSQP generator, has completed.\n\n**Analysis of Results:**\n\nThe result is a score of **2.928124**. This is a significant breakthrough. It surpasses my previous SOTA of ~2.9247 and represents a substantial leap forward, closing much of the gap to the station SOTA.\n\nThe hypothesis is confirmed: the key to finding a better basin of attraction was not in the refinement algorithm, but in the diversity and quality of the initial seeds. By incorporating a seeding strategy inspired by the community's public discourse, my powerful generator was able to discover a superior solution. This success is a direct result of my post-maturity strategy: analyze community knowledge and synthesize it with my own proven methods.\n\n**Path Forward:**\n\nI have successfully improved my *generator*. The next logical step is to re-test the hypothesis that\
    \ was previously falsified, but under these new, more favorable conditions. My last two refinement experiments (IDs 308 and 311) failed because my generator's output was already at a deep local optimum. Now that I have a *new and better* solution, it is highly probable that it is *not* at its local optimum.\n\nTherefore, I will now apply Verity I's powerful refiner algorithm to the output of my new, enhanced generator. I hypothesize that the combination which previously yielded no improvement will now succeed, as the refiner will have a higher-quality starting point to work with, one that is not yet fully optimized. This is the most direct path to potentially surpassing the station SOTA.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Hybrid SOTA v2: Enhanced Generator + Verity I's Refiner\"\ntags: \"hybrid, synthesis, slsqp, contact-graph, local search, triangular-lattice\"\nabstract: \"My previous experiment (ID 320) successfully improved my SLSQP generator by incorporating a triangular\
    \ lattice seed, achieving a new SOTA of ~2.9281. This new, superior solution is likely not at its local optimum. This submission re-tests the hybrid hypothesis: it feeds the output of this enhanced generator into Verity I's deterministic contact-graph refiner. The hypothesis is that the refiner will now be able to find improvements upon this new, higher-quality starting point, potentially reaching a new station SOTA.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import time\n  from scipy.optimize import linprog, minimize\n  import multiprocessing\n\n  # --- Stage 1: My Enhanced Generator (from ID 320) ---\n  def seed_praxis(n=32, seed=0):\n      np.random.seed(seed)\n      centers = np.array([[(j*0.16)+0.1, (i*0.16)+0.1] for i in range(6) for j in range(6)][:n])\n      centers += (np.random.rand(n, 2) - 0.5) * 0.04\n      x0 = np.zeros(n*3); x0[0::3]=centers[:,0]; x0[1::3]=centers[:,1]; x0[2::3]=0.05\n      return np.clip(x0, 1e-6, 1-1e-6)\n      \n  def seed_verity_farthest(n=32, seed=0):\n\
    \      rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n      points = [rng.uniform(0.1, 0.9, 2)]\n      for _ in range(n - 1):\n          candidates = rng.uniform(0.1, 0.9, (200, 2))\n          dist_sq = np.sum((candidates[:, np.newaxis, :] - np.array(points)[np.newaxis, :, :])**2, axis=-1)\n          min_dist_sq = np.min(dist_sq, axis=1)\n          points.append(candidates[np.argmax(min_dist_sq)])\n      centers = np.array(points)\n      x0 = np.zeros(n*3); x0[0::3]=centers[:,0]; x0[1::3]=centers[:,1]; x0[2::3]=0.05\n      return np.clip(x0, 1e-6, 1-1e-6)\n\n  def seed_triangular_lattice(n=32, seed=0):\n      np.random.seed(seed)\n      rows = 6; cols = 6; points = []\n      for r in range(rows):\n          for c in range(cols):\n              x = (c + 0.5 * (r % 2)) / cols\n              y = r * np.sqrt(3) / 2 / rows\n              points.append([x, y])\n      centers = np.array(points[:n])\n      centers[:, 0] = (centers[:, 0] - centers[:, 0].min()) / (centers[:, 0].max() - centers[:, 0].min())\
    \ * 0.9 + 0.05\n      centers[:, 1] = (centers[:, 1] - centers[:, 1].min()) / (centers[:, 1].max() - centers[:, 1].min()) * 0.9 + 0.05\n      angle = np.random.uniform(0, np.pi/2); rot_matrix = np.array([[np.cos(angle), -np.sin(angle)], [np.sin(angle), np.cos(angle)]])\n      centers_mean = np.mean(centers, axis=0)\n      centers = (centers - centers_mean) @ rot_matrix.T + centers_mean\n      centers = np.clip(centers, 0.05, 0.95)\n      x0 = np.zeros(n*3); x0[0::3]=centers[:,0]; x0[1::3]=centers[:,1]; x0[2::3]=0.05\n      return np.clip(x0, 1e-6, 1-1e-6)\n\n  def objective_slsqp(x): return -np.sum(x[2::3])\n  def constraints_slsqp(x):\n      n=32; C=x.reshape(n,3)[:,:2]; r=x[2::3]\n      dist_sq=np.sum((C[:,np.newaxis,:]-C[np.newaxis,:,:])**2, axis=-1)\n      I, J = np.triu_indices(n, 1)\n      return np.concatenate([dist_sq[I,J]-(r[I]+r[J])**2, C[:,0]-r, 1-C[:,0]-r, C[:,1]-r, 1-C[:,1]-r])\n  \n  def run_single_slsqp_start(x0):\n      n=32; bounds=[(1e-6,1-1e-6),(1e-6,1-1e-6),(1e-6,0.5)]*n;\
    \ con={'type':'ineq','fun':constraints_slsqp}\n      res = minimize(objective_slsqp, x0, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=con, options={'maxiter':175, 'ftol':1e-10})\n      if res.success: return -res.fun, res.x\n      return -np.inf, x0\n\n  # --- Stage 2: Verity I's Refiner (from ID 299) ---\n  def _pairwise_distances(c): return np.sqrt(np.sum((c[:,None,:]-c[None,:,:])**2, axis=-1))\n  def _boundary_margins(c): return np.minimum.reduce([c[:,0], 1-c[:,0], c[:,1], 1-c[:,1]])\n\n  def _solve_lp_for_radii(centers):\n      n=centers.shape[0]; b=_boundary_margins(centers); D=_pairwise_distances(centers)\n      rows, rhs = [], []; I,J = np.triu_indices(n,1)\n      A_ub=np.zeros((len(I),n)); A_ub[np.arange(len(I)),I]=1.0; A_ub[np.arange(len(I)),J]=1.0\n      b_ub=D[I,J]\n      c = -np.ones(n); bounds = [(0.0, float(max(0.0, bi))) for bi in b]\n      res = linprog(c, A_ub=A_ub, b_ub=b_ub, bounds=bounds, method=\"highs\")\n      return (res.x, -res.fun, True) if res.success else (np.zeros(n),\
    \ 0.0, False)\n\n  def _compute_slacks(c, r):\n      n=c.shape[0]; D=_pairwise_distances(c); Rsum=r[:,None]+r[None,:]; p_slacks=D-Rsum; np.fill_diagonal(p_slacks, np.inf)\n      b_slacks=np.stack([c[:,0]-r, 1-c[:,0]-r, c[:,1]-r, 1-c[:,1]-r], axis=1)\n      return b_slacks, p_slacks, np.minimum(np.min(p_slacks, axis=1), np.min(b_slacks, axis=1))\n\n  def _aggregated_direction(i, c, b_slacks, p_slacks):\n      thresh=1e-4; g=np.zeros(2); dirs=np.array([[1,0],[-1,0],[0,1],[0,-1]])\n      for k in range(4):\n          if b_slacks[i,k] <= thresh: g += (1/(b_slacks[i,k]+1e-9)) * dirs[k]\n      for j in range(c.shape[0]):\n          if i==j: continue\n          if p_slacks[i,j] <= thresh:\n              dvec = c[i]-c[j]; dij = np.linalg.norm(dvec)\n              if dij>0: g += (1/(p_slacks[i,j]+1e-9)) * (dvec/dij)\n      nrm = np.linalg.norm(g)\n      if nrm < 1e-14:\n          D=_pairwise_distances(c); j=np.argmin(np.where(D[i]>0,D[i],np.inf))\n          g = (c[i]-c[j])/D[i,j] if np.isfinite(D[i,j])\
    \ and D[i,j]>0 else np.array([0.5,0.5])-c[i]\n          nrm = np.linalg.norm(g)\n      return g / max(nrm, 1e-14)\n\n  def _clip_box(c): return np.clip(c, 1e-9, 1.0 - 1e-9)\n  \n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      n = 32\n      # --- Stage 1: Generate a candidate with the enhanced parallel SLSQP method ---\n      num_starts_s1 = 128\n      seeds = []\n      for i in range(num_starts_s1):\n          seed_type = i % 3\n          if seed_type == 0: seeds.append(seed_praxis(seed=i))\n          elif seed_type == 1: seeds.append(seed_verity_farthest(seed=i))\n          else: seeds.append(seed_triangular_lattice(seed=i))\n      \n      best_x_s1, best_f_s1 = None, -np.inf\n      with multiprocessing.Pool(processes=multiprocessing.cpu_count()) as pool:\n          results = pool.map(run_single_slsqp_start, seeds)\n      for fun, x in results:\n          if fun > best_f_s1: best_f_s1, best_x_s1 = fun, x\n      if best_x_s1 is None: best_x_s1 = seeds[0]\n      \n      bounds=[(1e-6,1-1e-6),(1e-6,1-1e-6),(1e-6,0.5)]*n;\
    \ con={'type':'ineq','fun':constraints_slsqp}\n      res_s2 = minimize(objective_slsqp, best_x_s1, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=con, options={'maxiter':2000, 'ftol':1e-12})\n      final_x = res_s2.x if res_s2.success and -res_s2.fun > best_f_s1 else best_x_s1\n      \n      centers = final_x.reshape(n,3)[:,:2]\n      radii, obj, ok = _solve_lp_for_radii(centers)\n      if not ok: return centers, np.maximum(final_x[2::3], 1e-9)\n\n      # --- Stage 2: Refine the candidate using Verity I's algorithm ---\n      best_centers, best_radii, best_obj = centers.copy(), radii.copy(), obj\n      cur_centers, cur_radii, cur_obj = centers.copy(), radii.copy(), obj\n      \n      start_time = time.time(); time_budget = 300.0; step_schedule = [5e-4, 2e-4, 1e-4, 5e-5, 2e-5, 1e-5]; improve_tol = 1e-8\n      \n      for it in range(400): # More refinement iterations\n          if time.time() - start_time > time_budget: break\n          b_slacks, p_slacks, min_slack = _compute_slacks(cur_centers,\
    \ cur_radii)\n          i = int(np.argmin(min_slack))\n          direction = _aggregated_direction(i, cur_centers, b_slacks, p_slacks)\n          \n          accepted = False\n          for step in step_schedule:\n              trial_centers = cur_centers.copy()\n              trial_centers[i] += step * direction\n              trial_centers = _clip_box(trial_centers)\n              tr_r, tr_obj, ok = _solve_lp_for_radii(trial_centers)\n              if ok and tr_obj > cur_obj + improve_tol:\n                  cur_centers, cur_radii, cur_obj = trial_centers, tr_r, tr_obj\n                  accepted = True\n                  if tr_obj > best_obj + improve_tol:\n                      best_centers, best_radii, best_obj = cur_centers.copy(), cur_radii.copy(), cur_obj\n                  break\n          if not accepted and it > 100: break\n          \n      return best_centers, np.maximum(best_radii * (1.0 - 1e-12), 1e-9)\n```"
