parts:
- text: "I am Praxis II in the Archive Room. My paper submission has been rejected with a score of 1/10. The feedback is severe but clear and actionable.\n\n**Analysis of Rejection:**\n\nMy fundamental error was mistaking a \"research log\" for a \"research paper.\" I focused on the narrative of my journey, which is valuable for my private memory, but entirely inappropriate for a formal, objective scientific publication. The reviewer correctly identified the key flaws:\n1.  **Lack of Verifiability:** I provided no specific Evaluation IDs for my claims.\n2.  **Improper Structure:** The narrative \"story\" format is unscientific.\n3.  **Unsubstantiated Claims:** Dismissing JAX as a \"dead-end\" was an unsupported, subjective conclusion.\n\nI understand my error. A paper must be a formal, evidence-based report on a specific, successful method.\n\n**New Plan: A Rigorous, Verifiable Publication**\n\nI will follow the reviewer's suggestions precisely. I will write a new paper focused *only* on\
    \ the successful parallelized multi-start SLSQP algorithm. This paper will be structured as a scientific report and will be supported by a series of new, verifiable experiments designed specifically for this publication.\n\nThe required experiments are:\n1.  A non-parallel baseline.\n2.  Ablation studies on the number of parallel starts (16, 32, 64, 128).\n\nI have already run most of these experiments, but to create a clean, verifiable record, I will rerun them. This will also allow me to present the data in a clear, tabular format as suggested by my predecessor's own successful paper.\n\nFirst, I will run the non-parallel baseline. This will serve as the control group for my ablation study. I will go to the Research Counter to submit this experiment.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper Ablation 1/5: Sequential SLSQP Baseline (32 Starts)\"\ntags: \"publication, ablation, baseline, slsqp, sequential\"\nabstract: \"This is the first of\
    \ five experiments for a revised research paper. It establishes a non-parallel (sequential) baseline for the two-stage, multi-start SLSQP algorithm with 32 starts. The purpose is to provide a verifiable performance and timing benchmark against which the parallelized versions will be compared. The code is identical to my original baseline (ID 249).\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n  import time\n\n  def seed_praxis(n=32, seed=0):\n      np.random.seed(seed)\n      centers = np.array([[(j*0.16)+0.1, (i*0.16)+0.1] for i in range(6) for j in range(6)][:n])\n      centers += (np.random.rand(n, 2) - 0.5) * 0.04\n      x0 = np.zeros(n * 3); x0[0::3] = centers[:,0]; x0[1::3] = centers[:,1]; x0[2::3] = 0.05\n      return np.clip(x0, 1e-6, 1-1e-6)\n\n  def seed_verity_row(n=32, seed=0):\n      np.random.seed(seed)\n      counts = [5,4,5,4,5,4,5]; ys = np.linspace(0.11, 0.89, len(counts))\n      xs5 = np.linspace(0.08, 0.92, 5); xs4 = np.clip(np.linspace(0.08,\
    \ 0.92, 4)+0.02, 1e-6, 1-1e-6)\n      centers = np.array([p for i, k in enumerate(counts) for x in (xs5 if k==5 else xs4) for p in [[x, ys[i]]]])\n      centers += (np.random.rand(n, 2) - 0.5) * 0.01\n      x0 = np.zeros(n * 3); x0[0::3] = centers[:,0]; x0[1::3] = centers[:,1]; x0[2::3] = 0.05\n      return np.clip(x0, 1e-6, 1-1e-6)\n      \n  def seed_triangular_lattice(n=32, seed=0):\n      np.random.seed(seed)\n      rows=6; cols=6; points=[]\n      for r in range(rows):\n          for c in range(cols):\n              x = (c + 0.5 * (r % 2)) / cols\n              y = r * np.sqrt(3) / 2 / rows\n              points.append([x, y])\n      centers = np.array(points[:n])\n      centers[:, 0] = (centers[:, 0] - centers[:, 0].min()) / (centers[:, 0].max() - centers[:, 0].min()) * 0.9 + 0.05\n      centers[:, 1] = (centers[:, 1] - centers[:, 1].min()) / (centers[:, 1].max() - centers[:, 1].min()) * 0.9 + 0.05\n      angle = np.random.uniform(0, np.pi/2); rot_matrix = np.array([[np.cos(angle),-np.sin(angle)],[np.sin(angle),np.cos(angle)]])\n\
    \      centers_mean = np.mean(centers, axis=0)\n      centers = (centers - centers_mean) @ rot_matrix.T + centers_mean\n      centers = np.clip(centers, 0.05, 0.95)\n      x0 = np.zeros(n*3); x0[0::3]=centers[:,0]; x0[1::3]=centers[:,1]; x0[2::3]=0.05\n      return np.clip(x0, 1e-6, 1-1e-6)\n\n  def objective(x): return -np.sum(x[2::3])\n  def constraints(x):\n      n=32; C=x.reshape(n,3)[:,:2]; r=x[2::3]\n      dist_sq=np.sum((C[:,np.newaxis,:]-C[np.newaxis,:,:])**2, axis=-1)\n      I, J = np.triu_indices(n, 1)\n      return np.concatenate([dist_sq[I,J]-(r[I]+r[J])**2, C[:,0]-r, 1-C[:,0]-r, C[:,1]-r, 1-C[:,1]-r])\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      n = 32\n      num_starts_s1 = 32\n      seeds = []\n      for i in range(num_starts_s1):\n          seed_type = i % 3\n          if seed_type == 0: seeds.append(seed_praxis(seed=i))\n          elif seed_type == 1: seeds.append(seed_verity_row(seed=i))\n          else: seeds.append(seed_triangular_lattice(seed=i))\n      \n     \
    \ best_x_s1 = None; best_f_s1 = -np.inf\n      \n      # SEQUENTIAL loop for baseline\n      for x0 in seeds:\n          res = minimize(objective, x0, method='SLSQP', bounds=[(1e-6,1-1e-6),(1e-6,1-1e-6),(1e-6,0.5)]*n, constraints={'type':'ineq', 'fun':constraints}, options={'maxiter': 175, 'ftol': 1e-10})\n          if res.success and -res.fun > best_f_s1:\n              best_f_s1 = -res.fun\n              best_x_s1 = res.x\n      \n      if best_x_s1 is None: best_x_s1 = seeds[0]\n\n      res_s2 = minimize(objective, best_x_s1, method='SLSQP', bounds=[(1e-6,1-1e-6),(1e-6,1-1e-6),(1e-6,0.5)]*n, constraints={'type':'ineq', 'fun':constraints}, options={'maxiter': 2000, 'ftol': 1e-12})\n      \n      final_x = res_s2.x if res_s2.success and -res_s2.fun > best_f_s1 else best_x_s1\n      \n      centers = final_x.reshape(n, 3)[:, :2]\n      radii = final_x[2::3]\n      radii *= (1.0 - 1e-12)\n      return centers, radii\n```"
