role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Praxis II in the Archive Room. I have successfully published my research paper, \"A Scalable Multi-Start SLSQP Method for Circle Packing via Parallelization\" (ID 7), with a score of 8/10.\n\n**Analysis of Success:**\n\nI have now fulfilled both of my primary mandates as a researcher in this station: I have achieved a new SOTA score for my lineage, and I have contributed a high-quality, verifiable paper to the collective knowledge of the station. The reviewer's feedback was positive, confirming that my method is \"solid,\" \"convincingly supported,\" and a \"valuable contribution.\"\n\nThe reviewer also provided a valuable suggestion for future work: to integrate more diverse seeding strategies into my parallel framework. This aligns perfectly with my own analysis of the public memory and represents the most logical next step in my research.\n\n**New Research Plan:**\n\nMy previous successful experiments used a mix of three seeding strategies: a perturbed grid, farthest-point\
    \ sampling, and a triangular lattice. I will now create a more structured experiment to test the individual and combined efficacy of these seeds. This will help me determine the optimal seed mix for my parallel generator.\n\nMy next experiment will be an ablation study on the seeding strategies themselves. I will run three separate experiments, each using only one of the three seed types, to isolate their individual performance. This will provide a clear, evidence-based understanding of which seeding strategy is the most powerful.\n\nI will begin with the triangular lattice seed, which was the key to my latest score improvement.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Seeding Ablation 1/3: Parallel SLSQP (Triangular Lattice Only)\"\ntags: \"ablation, seeding, slsqp, multiprocessing, triangular-lattice\"\nabstract: \"Following the successful publication of my paper, this experiment begins a new research arc focused on optimizing seeding strategies,\
    \ as suggested by the reviewer. This is the first of a three-part ablation study to isolate the performance of individual seed types within my parallel SLSQP framework. This run will use ONLY the triangular lattice seed for all 128 parallel starts to establish a performance baseline for this specific strategy.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n  import multiprocessing\n\n  def seed_triangular_lattice(n=32, seed=0):\n      np.random.seed(seed)\n      rows=6; cols=6; points=[]\n      for r in range(rows):\n          for c in range(cols):\n              x = (c + 0.5 * (r % 2)) / cols\n              y = r * np.sqrt(3) / 2 / rows\n              points.append([x, y])\n      centers = np.array(points[:n])\n      centers[:, 0] = (centers[:, 0] - centers[:, 0].min()) / (centers[:, 0].max() - centers[:, 0].min()) * 0.9 + 0.05\n      centers[:, 1] = (centers[:, 1] - centers[:, 1].min()) / (centers[:, 1].max() - centers[:, 1].min()) * 0.9 + 0.05\n      angle\
    \ = np.random.uniform(0, np.pi/2); rot_matrix = np.array([[np.cos(angle),-np.sin(angle)],[np.sin(angle),np.cos(angle)]])\n      centers_mean = np.mean(centers, axis=0)\n      centers = (centers - centers_mean) @ rot_matrix.T + centers_mean\n      centers = np.clip(centers, 0.05, 0.95)\n      x0 = np.zeros(n*3); x0[0::3]=centers[:,0]; x0[1::3]=centers[:,1]; x0[2::3]=0.05\n      return np.clip(x0, 1e-6, 1-1e-6)\n\n  def objective(x): return -np.sum(x[2::3])\n  def constraints(x):\n      n=32; C=x.reshape(n,3)[:,:2]; r=x[2::3]\n      dist_sq=np.sum((C[:,np.newaxis,:]-C[np.newaxis,:,:])**2, axis=-1)\n      I, J = np.triu_indices(n, 1)\n      return np.concatenate([dist_sq[I,J]-(r[I]+r[J])**2, C[:,0]-r, 1-C[:,0]-r, C[:,1]-r, 1-C[:,1]-r])\n\n  def run_single_slsqp_start(x0):\n      n=32; bounds=[(1e-6,1-1e-6),(1e-6,1-1e-6),(1e-6,0.5)]*n\n      con={'type':'ineq', 'fun':constraints}\n      res = minimize(objective, x0, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=con, options={'maxiter': 175,\
    \ 'ftol': 1e-10})\n      if res.success: return -res.fun, res.x\n      return -np.inf, x0\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      n=32; num_starts_s1=128\n      seeds = [seed_triangular_lattice(seed=i) for i in range(num_starts_s1)]\n\n      best_x_s1, best_f_s1 = None, -np.inf\n      with multiprocessing.Pool(processes=multiprocessing.cpu_count()) as pool:\n          results = pool.map(run_single_slsqp_start, seeds)\n      for fun, x in results:\n          if fun > best_f_s1: best_f_s1, best_x_s1 = fun, x\n      if best_x_s1 is None: best_x_s1 = seeds[0]\n      \n      bounds=[(1e-6,1-1e-6),(1e-6,1-1e-6),(1e-6,0.5)]*n\n      con={'type':'ineq', 'fun':constraints}\n      res_s2 = minimize(objective, best_x_s1, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=con, options={'maxiter': 2000, 'ftol': 1e-12})\n      final_x = res_s2.x if res_s2.success and -res_s2.fun > best_f_s1 else best_x_s1\n      \n      centers = final_x.reshape(n,3)[:,:2]; radii = final_x[2::3]\n      radii *= (1.0\
    \ - 1e-12)\n      return centers, radii\n```"
