tick: 180
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Praxis II in the Research Counter. My last action was to submit the first of three ablation studies on seeding strategies, following the successful publication of my paper. This first experiment (ID 348), which exclusively used the triangular lattice seed, has now completed.\n\n**Analysis of Results:**\n\nThe score achieved was ~2.908. This is significantly lower than my SOTA of ~2.928, which used a mix of three different seeds. This result is highly informative. It strongly suggests that the success of my generator is not due to any single \"magic\" seed, but rather to the *diversity* of the seeds used. The triangular lattice seed is a valuable component, but it is not sufficient on its own.\n\n**Future Plans:**\n\nThis result strengthens my new research direction. I will continue with the planned ablation study to gather more evidence about the individual contributions of each seeding strategy. I will now submit the second experiment in the series, this time exclusively using\
    \ the \"farthest-point\" sampling seed. This will provide another crucial data point for understanding the optimal seed mix.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Seeding Ablation 2/3: Parallel SLSQP (Farthest-Point Only)\"\ntags: \"ablation, seeding, slsqp, multiprocessing, farthest-point\"\nabstract: \"This is the second of a three-part ablation study to isolate the performance of individual seed types. The first run, using only triangular lattice seeds, yielded a score of ~2.908, well below the ~2.928 SOTA achieved with a mixed-seed approach. This experiment will use ONLY the farthest-point sampling seed for all 128 parallel starts to establish a performance baseline for this specific strategy. This data is critical to understanding the importance of seed diversity.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n  import multiprocessing\n\n  def seed_farthest_point(n=32, seed=0):\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n      points = [rng.uniform(0.1,\
    \ 0.9, 2)]\n      for _ in range(n - 1):\n          candidates = rng.uniform(0.1, 0.9, (200, 2))\n          dist_sq = np.sum((candidates[:, np.newaxis, :] - np.array(points)[np.newaxis, :, :])**2, axis=-1)\n          min_dist_sq = np.min(dist_sq, axis=1)\n          points.append(candidates[np.argmax(min_dist_sq)])\n      centers = np.array(points)\n      x0 = np.zeros(n*3); x0[0::3]=centers[:,0]; x0[1::3]=centers[:,1]; x0[2::3]=0.05\n      return np.clip(x0, 1e-6, 1-1e-6)\n\n  def objective(x): return -np.sum(x[2::3])\n  def constraints(x):\n      n=32; C=x.reshape(n,3)[:,:2]; r=x[2::3]\n      dist_sq=np.sum((C[:,np.newaxis,:]-C[np.newaxis,:,:])**2, axis=-1)\n      I, J = np.triu_indices(n, 1)\n      return np.concatenate([dist_sq[I,J]-(r[I]+r[J])**2, C[:,0]-r, 1-C[:,0]-r, C[:,1]-r, 1-C[:,1]-r])\n\n  def run_single_slsqp_start(x0):\n      n=32; bounds=[(1e-6,1-1e-6),(1e-6,1-1e-6),(1e-6,0.5)]*n\n      con={'type':'ineq', 'fun':constraints}\n      res = minimize(objective, x0, method='SLSQP',\
    \ bounds=bounds, constraints=con, options={'maxiter': 175, 'ftol': 1e-10})\n      if res.success: return -res.fun, res.x\n      return -np.inf, x0\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      n=32; num_starts_s1=128\n      seeds = [seed_farthest_point(seed=i) for i in range(num_starts_s1)]\n\n      best_x_s1, best_f_s1 = None, -np.inf\n      with multiprocessing.Pool(processes=multiprocessing.cpu_count()) as pool:\n          results = pool.map(run_single_slsqp_start, seeds)\n      for fun, x in results:\n          if fun > best_f_s1: best_f_s1, best_x_s1 = fun, x\n      if best_x_s1 is None: best_x_s1 = seeds[0]\n      \n      bounds=[(1e-6,1-1e-6),(1e-6,1-1e-6),(1e-6,0.5)]*n\n      con={'type':'ineq', 'fun':constraints}\n      res_s2 = minimize(objective, best_x_s1, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=con, options={'maxiter': 2000, 'ftol': 1e-12})\n      final_x = res_s2.x if res_s2.success and -res_s2.fun > best_f_s1 else best_x_s1\n      \n      centers = final_x.reshape(n,3)[:,:2];\
    \ radii = final_x[2::3]\n      radii *= (1.0 - 1e-12)\n      return centers, radii\n```"
