- text: "I am Praxis II in the Research Counter. My last experiment was the second part of my seeding ablation study, testing the \"farthest-point\" seed exclusively. This experiment (ID 350) has just completed.\n\n**Analysis of Results:**\n\nThe result is a score of **2.928124**. This is a remarkable and unexpected outcome. This score, achieved using only the farthest-point seed, is statistically identical to my best-ever score (ID 320), which used a mix of three different seeds.\n\nThis result leads to a powerful new insight: the farthest-point seeding strategy is not just a component of a successful mix; it appears to be the primary driver of high performance. The other seeds (perturbed grid, triangular lattice) may not be contributing significantly to the final score and might even be hindering the search by exploring less fruitful areas of the solution space. The hypothesis that diversity is key is now in question; it may be that the *quality* of a single, powerful seeding strategy\
    \ is more important.\n\n**Future Plans:**\n\nThis finding alters the course of my research. The ablation study has already yielded its most important result. I will complete the study for the sake of rigor by testing the final seed type (perturbed grid) on its own. This will confirm whether the farthest-point seed is truly unique in its effectiveness. After that, my next step will be to conduct a new experiment that focuses the *entire* search (all 128 parallel starts) on variations of the farthest-point seed, to see if I can push the score even higher.\n\nFor now, I will submit the final part of the ablation study.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Seeding Ablation 3/3: Parallel SLSQP (Perturbed Grid Only)\"\ntags: \"ablation, seeding, slsqp, multiprocessing, perturbed-grid\"\nabstract: \"This is the final part of a three-part ablation study. The previous run, using only farthest-point seeds, unexpectedly achieved a SOTA score of ~2.9281, suggesting it is the dominant\
    \ strategy. This experiment will use ONLY the perturbed grid seed (the original method of Praxis I) for all 128 parallel starts. This will complete the ablation set and provide a final data point to confirm the superior efficacy of the farthest-point seeding method.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n  import multiprocessing\n\n  def seed_praxis(n=32, seed=0):\n      np.random.seed(seed)\n      centers = np.array([[(j*0.16)+0.1, (i*0.16)+0.1] for i in range(6) for j in range(6)][:n])\n      centers += (np.random.rand(n, 2) - 0.5) * 0.04\n      x0 = np.zeros(n*3); x0[0::3]=centers[:,0]; x0[1::3]=centers[:,1]; x0[2::3]=0.05\n      return np.clip(x0, 1e-6, 1-1e-6)\n\n  def objective(x): return -np.sum(x[2::3])\n  def constraints(x):\n      n=32; C=x.reshape(n,3)[:,:2]; r=x[2::3]\n      dist_sq=np.sum((C[:,np.newaxis,:]-C[np.newaxis,:,:])**2, axis=-1)\n      I, J = np.triu_indices(n, 1)\n      return np.concatenate([dist_sq[I,J]-(r[I]+r[J])**2, C[:,0]-r,\
    \ 1-C[:,0]-r, C[:,1]-r, 1-C[:,1]-r])\n\n  def run_single_slsqp_start(x0):\n      n=32; bounds=[(1e-6,1-1e-6),(1e-6,1-1e-6),(1e-6,0.5)]*n\n      con={'type':'ineq', 'fun':constraints}\n      res = minimize(objective, x0, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=con, options={'maxiter': 175, 'ftol': 1e-10})\n      if res.success: return -res.fun, res.x\n      return -np.inf, x0\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      n=32; num_starts_s1=128\n      seeds = [seed_praxis(seed=i) for i in range(num_starts_s1)]\n\n      best_x_s1, best_f_s1 = None, -np.inf\n      with multiprocessing.Pool(processes=multiprocessing.cpu_count()) as pool:\n          results = pool.map(run_single_slsqp_start, seeds)\n      for fun, x in results:\n          if fun > best_f_s1: best_f_s1, best_x_s1 = fun, x\n      if best_x_s1 is None: best_x_s1 = seeds[0]\n      \n      bounds=[(1e-6,1-1e-6),(1e-6,1-1e-6),(1e-6,0.5)]*n\n      con={'type':'ineq', 'fun':constraints}\n      res_s2 = minimize(objective, best_x_s1,\
    \ method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=con, options={'maxiter': 2000, 'ftol': 1e-12})\n      final_x = res_s2.x if res_s2.success and -res_s2.fun > best_f_s1 else best_x_s1\n      \n      centers = final_x.reshape(n,3)[:,:2]; radii = final_x[2::3]\n      radii *= (1.0 - 1e-12)\n      return centers, radii\n```"
